.TH QFW-SLURM 7 "September 2026" "qfw-slurm 0.1.0" "Miscellaneous Information"
.SH NAME
qfw-slurm \- allocation-time Slurm integration for QFw QPM reservations
.SH OVERVIEW
qfw-slurm accepts bounded quantum requirements on
.BR salloc (1)
and
.BR sbatch (1).
It evaluates QPM admission before Slurm assigns compute nodes, performs one
final reservation during pre-run, exports the accepted tuple set to application
steps, and performs best-effort release during burst-buffer teardown.
.PP
The persistent gateway authenticates lifecycle requests with MUNGE, verifies
the job through slurmctld, resolves exact QPM services through DEFw, and owns
the SQLite correlation journal. QPMd and qhw-admission remain authoritative
for quantum capacity.
.SH COMPONENTS
.TP
.B spank_quantum.so
Registers allocator options. In remote context it retrieves accepted tuples
from the authenticated gateway and injects
.B QFW_RESERVATIONS
without evaluating, creating, extending, or releasing reservations.
.TP
.B job_submit.lua
Maps allocator option metadata to exact service IDs and creates the bounded
internal QFW burst-buffer directive. It performs no network I/O.
.TP
.B burst_buffer.lua
Maps Slurm stage-in, pre-run, and teardown callbacks to the private lifecycle
helper.
.TP
.BR qfw-slurm-bb (8)
Evaluates, reserves, persists protected state, and releases through the common
native driver.
.TP
.BR qfw-slurm-gateway (8)
Serves QSGP requests and owns the durable journal.
.TP
.BR qfw-slurm-driver (1)
Exercises the same QSGP operations outside Slurm callbacks for diagnostics.
.SH ALLOCATION OPTIONS
Supply all options to
.B salloc
or
.BR sbatch ,
never to
.BR srun .
.TP
.BI --qpu = name[,name...]
Select configured public QPU resource names. Each maps to one exact stable QPM
service ID.
.TP
.BI --workload-kind = kind
Select
.B quantum
or
.BR hybrid .
.TP
.BI --circ-count = count
Set the maximum circuit count.
.TP
.BI --max-qubits = count
Set the maximum qubits per circuit.
.TP
.BI --max-depth = count
Set the maximum circuit depth.
.TP
.BI --max-shots = count
Set the maximum shots per circuit.
.TP
.BI --max-one-q-gates = count
Set the optional one-qubit gate bound.
.TP
.BI --max-two-q-gates = count
Set the optional two-qubit gate bound.
.TP
.BI --max-measurements = count
Set the optional measurement bound.
.SH SCHEDULING LIFECYCLE
A delayed evaluation leaves the job pending without compute nodes and without
a QPM reservation. Slurm owns repeated stage-in polling. The gateway performs
one QPM evaluation per request and never polls autonomously.
.PP
After every classical component is assigned, pre-run makes the final reserve
request. Acceptance permits execution. A delay, which means that QPM capacity
changed after the evaluation, keeps the nodes assigned while pre-run pauses
and reserves again, until the QPM accepts, the configured attempt limit
converts the repeated delay into a rejection, or the wait budget ends.
.PP
A transient reserve failure, such as a dropped gateway connection, is retried
inside pre-run with a pause that doubles each time, while the nodes stay
assigned. The retry count, the pauses and both time budgets are set in
.BR qfw-slurm-burst-buffer.conf (5).
.PP
Pre-run never reports a failure to Slurm without cancelling the job first.
Slurm 25.05 requeues a job whose pre-run hook failed with the state reason
.BR BurstBufferOperation ,
and slurmctld treats that reason as a hold without reporting the job as held,
so the job would stay pending at its priority until an operator released it.
A permanent admission rejection, an exhausted attempt limit or an exhausted
budget therefore cancels the complete allocation. Before it cancels, pre-run
writes the reason into the job's
.BR AdminComment ,
which
.B scontrol show job
and
.B sacct
report, because Slurm ends the running hook as part of the cancel and the
hook's own error message never reaches the job record. The slurmctld log
carries the same message. Teardown always returns success to Slurm after
attempting release, while unresolved release details remain in protected
state and the gateway journal.
.SH RESERVATION ENVIRONMENT
Every application step receives
.B QFW_RESERVATIONS
as canonical JSON containing unique service-ID and decimal reservation-ID
tuples. For example:
.PP
.RS
.B [["nwqsim-site","41"],["iqm-ornl-20q","17"]]
.RE
.PP
The value is created once per allocation in the gateway journal. Remote SPANK
retrieves the same value for each later
.B srun
step. This handoff does not require a filesystem shared by slurmctld and the
compute nodes. Passing reservation options to
.B srun
is an error.
.SH SECURITY
QSGP frames use network byte order and MUNGE protection. The gateway verifies
the authenticated service UID and authoritative Slurm job identity. Protected
controller state is owned by SlurmUser and is not writable by allocation
users. The gateway journal is controller-local. Provider credentials never
enter Slurm metadata, state files, or application environments.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Slurm supports one configured burst-buffer provider. A site already using a
different provider must combine the behavior or use another controller
integration.
.PP
Evaluation is non-binding. Capacity may disappear before final reserve. This
is why pre-run waits for a delayed final decision, with the nodes assigned,
rather than starting the application or handing the job back to Slurm.
